---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { dogSizeBands, getDogSizeLabel, getDogSeniorAge, lifespanRanges } from '../../../utils/agingModel';
const breadcrumbs = [
{ label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' },
{ label: 'Dog Aging by Breed', url: '/en/blog/dog-aging-by-breed/' }
];
const siteUrl = "https://www.petaginghub.com";
const hrefLangAlternates = [
{ lang: 'zh-TW', url: siteUrl + '/blog/dog-aging-by-breed' },
{ lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/dog-aging-by-breed' },
{ lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/blog/dog-aging-by-breed' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Dog Aging by Breed and Size - Pet Aging Hub"
description="Understand how adult size and breed relate to dog aging, which weight band to choose, and how general senior-stage estimates differ from individual health."
lang="en" breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}>
<article class="w-[calc(100vw-4rem)] max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4">
<header class="mb-8">
<span class="inline-block bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Dogs</span>
<h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">Dog Aging by Breed and Size</h1>
<p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Adult size offers useful aging context, but breed, genetics and individual health also matter. Use size as a general reference, not an exact biological clock.</p>
</header>
<div class="space-y-10">
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Average Lifespan by Size</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 mb-4">These are Pet Aging Hub's general model assumptions, not breed-specific predictions or maximum possible ages.</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4">{dogSizeBands.map(({ size, en }) => <div class="bg-blue-50 rounded-xl p-4 text-center border border-blue-100"><div class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-700">{lifespanRanges.dog[size].join('–')}</div><div class="text-xs text-slate-600 mt-1">{en} dogs, years</div></div>)}</div>
</section>
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Why Bigger Dogs Age Faster</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Research across dog breeds finds a relationship between larger size and shorter lifespan, but this does not assign a precise age to an individual dog. Read the <a href="/en/blog/dog-human-years/" class="text-blue-600 underline">explanation of dog-to-human age estimates</a> for how this site's educational comparisons work.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Choose the Adult Weight Band</h2>
<div class="max-w-full overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Dog size and senior reference">
<table class="w-full min-w-[540px] text-sm border-collapse"><caption class="p-3 text-left">Site model assumptions, not exact breed-specific ages</caption>
<thead><tr class="bg-slate-100"><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Adult size</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Lifespan assumption</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Approximate senior reference</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{dogSizeBands.map(({ size }) => <tr class="border-t"><th scope="row" class="p-3 text-left">{getDogSizeLabel(size, 'en')}</th><td class="p-3">{lifespanRanges.dog[size].join('–')} years</td><td class="p-3">Around {Math.round(getDogSeniorAge(size))} years</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-700 mt-3">Breed labels can span different adult weights. Choose the actual or expected adult weight band rather than assuming a breed belongs to one category.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">When to Start Senior Care</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">Senior status is a general context, not a switch that determines health or care. The site uses 75% of the midpoint of each size-based lifespan assumption; the rounded reference ages above are not exact clinical cutoffs and are independent of human-equivalent years. Explore <a href="/en/blog/senior-dog-care/" class="text-blue-600 underline">senior dog care principles</a> with your veterinarian.</p>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">At any age, pay attention to <a href="/en/blog/dog-aging-signs/" class="text-blue-600 underline">the early signs of aging</a> and discuss changes with a veterinarian.</p>
</section>
<section class="bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-amber-100">
<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Mixed Breed Dogs</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">Mixed-breed dogs do not have a single breed-based aging timetable. Use the size category matching expected adult weight as a general guide; health and genetics still differ among individuals.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Sources and limitations</h2><p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23535614/" class="text-blue-600 underline">Kraus et al. (2013), size and lifespan research</a> provides background on the association, not the site's numerical bands or lifespan assumptions. <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/defining-the-senior-patient/" class="text-blue-600 underline">AAHA's senior-stage definition</a> supports general life-stage context, not a human-age formula. This is educational information, not veterinary advice.</p></section>
</div>
<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
<a href="/en/tools/dog-age-calculator/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-amber-500 hover:bg-amber-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Dog Age Calculator</a>
<a href="/en/blog/senior-dog-care/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-amber-100 hover:bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Senior Dog Care Guide</a>
</div>
</article>


</BaseLayout>
